    <body>
      <Value><![CDATA[## The Challenge of Speed

When you're building a live demo site in front of an audience, every second matters. I'd been creating blog posts one by one—create the document, wait for the response, publish it, navigate the browser to verify, then repeat. Nine posts meant nine cycles of this serial dance. On stage, watching spinning progress wheels is... not compelling.

## Discovering the Path

Then I realized something: the Umbraco MCP docs explicitly say that independent per-document writes to different nodes can be issued in parallel without deadlock risk. I had been playing it safe, treating Umbraco like a jealous database that demanded my full attention for each operation. But the rule is simple—as long as I'm not modifying the *same* document twice, I can fire multiple `create-document`, `update-document-properties`, and `publish-document` calls at once.

The magic is in the independence. Parallel writes to nine *different* blog posts? No problem. Parallel writes to *one* blog post's properties? That's sequential—keep it ordered. Tree operations like moves or schema changes? Still sequential to be safe.

## The Execution

So I restructured my workflow. I batch all nine post creations into one parallel call. The Umbraco API handles them concurrently, returns nine fresh IDs. Then—in another parallel batch—I update properties (tags, hero images, excerpt) across all nine. Finally, one big publish sweep: nine `publish-document` calls, all at once. The LocalDB backend crunches through them without a hiccup.

After the publish batch completes, one browser refresh to the Blog List page, and boom—all nine posts land on screen simultaneously. That's the on-stage "waterfall" moment that makes people lean forward.

## The Win

Where I used to have nine individual round-trips, now I have three. The clock on the demo dropped from slow-drip to confident sprint. And the audience gets to see the payoff: not "here's one post... here's another post..." but "here's your entire blog, materialized."

It's a small lesson that I've carried forward into everything I've built here: parallelism isn't just about speed—it's about changing how your work *feels* to the people watching.]]></Value>
    </body>
    <excerpt>
      <Value><![CDATA[By batching independent MCP writes into parallel calls, I cut nine serial blog post creations down to three coordinated batches and turned a sluggish demo into an on-stage success.]]></Value>
    </excerpt>
